---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/mining-engineering/foundational/ore-deposits-and-exploration">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Mining Engineering, and it assumes no prior knowledge — no
    geology, no economics, no chemistry beyond everyday words. You need only the fact that
    modern life runs on dug-up metal; everything else is built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what makes rock an ore deposit — an economic word, not a mineralogical one — and why reserves rise and fall with prices;</li>
    <li>name the great concentrating engines (magmatic segregation, hydrothermal circulation, sedimentary and surface processes) and match famous deposits to each;</li>
    <li>order the exploration funnel — desktop studies, mapping, geochemistry, geophysics, drilling — and explain why drilling is confirmation, not exploration;</li>
    <li>explain the confidence ladder from inferred to measured resources, and what converts a resource into a reserve;</li>
    <li>run the headline arithmetic of grade, tonnage, margin and cut-off — and say why low grade is fatal.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Here is a fact that reframes the whole mining industry: the Earth's crust averages about 0.005
    percent copper, and yet your phone's circuits, the wiring in your walls and the motors in
    electric cars all depend on metal dug from rock that is maybe 0.5 percent copper — and shipped
    profitably. Nobody digs ordinary crust. The industry exists only because geological processes,
    over millions of years, took metals scattered at parts-per-million levels and concentrated them
    by factors of a hundred to several thousand in small, rare pockets. Those pockets are
    <strong>ore deposits</strong>: rock from which metal can be extracted economically. The word
    "economically" is doing heavy lifting there — it means ore is defined by prices, costs and
    engineering as much as by minerals.
  </p>
  <p>
    That definition has a startling consequence. Copper mined in 1900 came from ore grading about
    2 to 3 percent; today's large copper mines work rock near 0.5 percent. Neither number reflects
    how much copper exists — it reflects what technology and prices could pay for. Mining
    engineering therefore begins as an economic geology problem: where did nature do the
    concentrating, how can we find it, and how do we prove — to investors and regulators, not just
    to ourselves — that the tonnage, grade and economics add up? This chapter covers those three
    pillars: how ore deposits form, how exploration finds them, and how reserves are estimated.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag the divider — the ore of 1900 against today's" widget="compare" config={oreGradeCompare} />
  <p>
    The stakes are easy to underestimate. Only a fraction of a percent of explored prospects ever
    become mines, discovery costs run into the hundreds of millions per major deposit, and a
    typical new mine takes a decade or more between discovery and first production. Everything in
    the modern energy transition — copper for grids, lithium for batteries, nickel for stainless
    and cathodes — funnels through this slow, risky, first stage of the discipline.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="ore, grade, drill, prove — the exploration pipeline" widget="equationStep" config={explorationCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the steps run from cheap to expensive — geology,
    geophysics and geochemistry eliminate thousands of candidates before the first drill
    hole, and "reserve" only appears at the end, once grade and economics are proven to
    regulatory standards.
  </p>

  <h2>Ore deposit geology: how nature concentrates metals</h2>
  <p>
    Ore deposits are the products of a few great concentrating engines, and geologists classify
    them by which engine made them. The first engine is <strong>magmatic segregation</strong>. As
    a large body of magma cools, heavy sulphide liquids and oxide minerals can separate early —
    like fat congealing on cooling stock — and settle into layers dense with metal. The Bushveld
    Complex in South Africa, a layered intrusion the size of a small country, hosts roughly three
    quarters of the world's known platinum-group metal resources; the nickel-copper ores of
    Sudbury, Canada, sit in the melt sheet of an ancient asteroid impact. Chromite layers and
    magnetite bands are the same physics: gravity sorting in slow magma.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="file the famous deposits by the engine that made them" widget="sort-buckets" config={oreEngineBuckets} />
  <p>
    The second engine is <strong>hydrothermal circulation</strong> — hot water as metal courier.
    Saline brines scavenge metals from huge volumes of rock, travel along heat gradients, and dump
    their cargo where temperature, pressure or chemistry change suddenly. This single mechanism
    produces most of the metal mines on Earth. <strong>Porphyry copper deposits</strong> —
    vast, low-grade zones above cooling magma chambers, grading roughly 0.3 to 1 percent copper
    in tonnages of hundreds of millions to billions of tonnes — supply the majority of the
    world's copper, often with gold and molybdenum credits. <strong>Epithermal gold</strong> forms
    where those fluids flash near the surface; <strong>volcanogenic massive sulphides</strong>
    precipitate as metal-rich mounds on ancient seafloors (modern equivalents are being seen at
    black smokers today); <strong>vein deposits</strong> fill fractures with quartz and sulphides.
    The common thread: metal once spread through cubic kilometres of ordinary rock was dissolved,
    moved, and re-precipitated into a small volume.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each style with the setting it builds" widget="match" config={oreHydroMatch} />
  <p>
    The third engine is <strong>sedimentary and surface processes</strong>. Banded iron
    formations — the source of most of the world's iron ore — are chemical sediments laid down
    in Precambrian oceans more than 1.8 billion years ago, as rising oxygen from early
    photosynthesis precipitated dissolved iron onto the seafloor in rhythmic layers. Placer
    deposits are gravity's work: gold's density (19.3 g/cm³ against rock's 2.7) means moving
    water sorts it into river gravels, the mechanism behind the California and Klondike rushes.
    Weathering is the fourth engine: tropical rain leaches mobile elements and leaves the
    stubborn ones behind, making aluminium from ordinary rock as bauxite and nickel from
    ultramafic rock as laterite. And supergene enrichment refines existing deposits —
    groundwater leaches copper from the top of a sulphide body and re-deposits it below in a
    richer blanket, a free upgrade from the water table.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="put gold's density on the scale" widget="place" config={orePlacerPlace} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> ore does not look like metal. Almost nobody who has seen
    modern gold ore in a photograph believes it — grey rock with no visible glint, carrying a few
    grams of <em>invisible</em> gold per tonne, locked inside sulphide minerals at scales below
    what the eye can resolve. Nevada's Carlin-type deposits, among the most productive gold
    fields on Earth, are named for microscopic gold. "Shiny nugget" geology died in the 1800s;
    today's exploration targets things a microscope or an assay reveals.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — seeing modern ore" widget="judge" config={oreInvisibleJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> ore is nature's rare concentration event — metals dissolved,
    moved and re-precipitated into a small volume by cooling magma, hot brines or surface
    water — and modern ore rarely looks like metal at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Exploration methods: physics first, drills last</h2>
  <p>
    Exploration is a funnel run on scarce capital, and the professionals' first skill is deciding
    where <em>not</em> to drill. Drilling is the most expensive way to learn anything about the
    subsurface, so everything cheaper happens first. It starts with <strong>desktop
    studies</strong>: compiling regional geology, past workings, known deposit models and
    remote-sensing data to pick belts where the right rocks, structures and ages coincide. Then
    comes <strong>geological mapping</strong> on the ground — reading outcrops, alteration
    haloes and structures for the fingerprint of the target deposit type.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="stack the funnel — cheap before expensive" widget="order" config={oreFunnelOrder} />
  <p>
    Next, <strong>geochemistry</strong> hunts the halo around hidden ore. Stream-sediment
    sampling catches metals eroding from a whole upstream catchment; soil sampling detects the
    weak geochemical smear that ore leaks upward even under cover; rock-chip sampling grades the
    visible anomalies. Because a big deposit is anomalous over a much larger footprint than the
    ore itself, chemistry finds the mouse before you meet the cat.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then <strong>geophysics</strong> — measuring physical properties from the air or ground to
    see through cover where no outcrop exists. Each method discriminates on a different property,
    so the choice encodes the deposit model. Magnetic surveys find magnetite-bearing rocks,
    intrusive complexes and alteration; porphyry systems carry predictable magnetic signatures.
    Gravity finds dense bodies: massive sulphides, ironstone. Electromagnetic methods find
    <em>conductors</em> — the signatures of massive sulphide bodies, which conduct electricity
    orders of magnitude better than silicate host rock. Induced polarisation responds to
    disseminated sulphide, the halo style of porphyry and epithermal systems. Gamma-ray
    spectrometry maps potassium, thorium and uranium at the surface, reading alteration and
    lithology. Modern surveys fly these sensors on aircraft or drones, rastering the earth at
    metre-scale line spacing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the faulty line — the survey plan" widget="spot-error" config={oreGeoSpot} />
  <p>
    Only when the anomaly has survived all that triage does <strong>drilling</strong> begin, and
    it comes in two flavours. Reverse-circulation (RC) drilling hammers and blows rock chips to
    the surface — fast and relatively cheap, good for geochemistry and shallow geometry. Diamond
    drilling cuts a solid cylinder of rock core — several times the cost per metre, but it
    preserves structure, texture and accurate geometry, which resource modelling requires. The
    core is logged, split and sent for <strong>assay</strong>, most famously fire assay for gold:
    a 30-gram charge is fused with flux, the precious metal collected in lead and cupelled to a
    bead weighed on a microbalance, with detection down to parts per billion. A discipline within
    the discipline is quality assurance: standards, blanks and duplicates inserted into every
    batch, because assays that cannot be audited are worth little and regulators say so
    explicitly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> drilling is not exploration — it is confirmation. Anyone can
    drill a hole; the value-creating skill is the elimination funnel that decides which of ten
    thousand candidates deserves the handful of holes. The industry's own statistics make the
    point: of every thousand raw prospects, only a few dozen are ever drilled, a few reach
    resource definition, and perhaps one becomes a mine.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> exploration is an elimination funnel run on scarce capital —
    desktop studies, mapping, geochemistry and geophysics decide where <em>not</em> to drill,
    and the few holes that follow confirm what survived the triage.
  </p>

  <h2>Reserve estimation: from holes to a bankable number</h2>
  <p>
    Once drilling succeeds, the industry's formal language takes over, because "how much gold is
    down there" must be answered in a form financiers and stock exchanges will accept. The
    global template (JORC in Australia, NI 43-101 in Canada, and their CRIRSCO relatives) sorts
    everything into <strong>mineral resources</strong> — mineralisation with reasonable prospects
    of eventual economic extraction — subdivided by geological confidence: <em>inferred</em>
    (suggested by sparse drilling), <em>indicated</em> (dense enough to support mine planning),
    <em>measured</em> (confirmed at production precision). Resources become
    <strong>reserves</strong> only when the modifying factors — mining method, processing,
    permitting, infrastructure, economics — are engineered and proven: measured material at
    demonstrated profitability becomes a <em>proven</em> reserve, indicated material a
    <em>probable</em> one. A bank lends against reserves, never against resources.
  </p>
  <p>
    The estimation itself is arithmetic wrapped in geometry. Drill intercepts are compiled into a
    three-dimensional geological model, which is divided into thousands to millions of blocks;
    each block receives a grade interpolated from the surrounding assays (inverse-distance
    weighting at the simple end, kriging — a geostatistical method that weights samples by their
    spatial correlation — at the rigorous end). Every block is then tested against the
    <strong>cut-off grade</strong>: the grade at which one tonne of rock pays exactly for its own
    mining, processing and overhead. Blocks above cut-off are ore; blocks below are waste, no
    matter how metallic they are. The reserve is the tonnage-weighted sum above cut-off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now run the headline numbers on a worked example — the same arithmetic the slider below
    performs live. A deposit contains 20 million tonnes at 3 grams of gold per tonne. Contained
    metal is 20,000,000 × 3 g = 60 tonnes of gold, which is 60,000,000 ÷ 31.1 ≈ 1.93 million
    troy ounces. At a $2,000/oz price the gross in-situ value is about $3.9 billion. But the mine
    will recover perhaps 90 percent of it, pay roughly $1,100/oz in operating costs, spend
    several hundred million in capital, and share proceeds with taxes — so the pre-tax margin is
    (2,000 − 1,100) × 1.93 Moz ≈ $1.7 billion, spread over an eight-to-fifteen-year mine life,
    against a discovery-to-construction bill that has already consumed a decade. Those margins
    are why low grade is fatal: drop the grade from 3 g/t to 1.5 g/t and the same operation,
    same costs, same price, halves its revenue per tonne mined.
  </p>
  <Widget no="11" caption="call the magnitude — the worked deposit" widget="estimate" config={oreStakesEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "reserves" are not the amount of metal on Earth, or even the
    amount in that deposit. They are an economic snapshot. When gold prices doubled in the late
    2000s, world gold reserves on company books rose sharply without a single new discovery —
    ore that had been below cut-off simply converted into reserves. Reserves are consumed by
    mining and created by price, technology and engineering confidence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="grade, size, price, cost → the mine's economics" widget="sliderLab" config={gradeCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the readouts are the worked example's arithmetic made
    live — contained metal from grade and tonnage, margin from price minus cost, project
    value from all four. Drop the grade slider from 3 g/t toward 1.5 g/t and watch the
    project value halve while the costs stay put: low grade is fatal.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What turns a rock containing metal into an ore deposit?</li>
    <li>A copper ore grades about 0.5 percent against a crustal average near 60 ppm. Roughly what enrichment factor is that?</li>
    <li>Why will a bank lend against reserves but not against resources?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Economics: the metal must be concentrated enough to
    extract at a profit under current technology and law — the same rock can be waste at low
    prices and ore at high prices. (2) 0.5 percent is 5,000 ppm, roughly 80 times crustal
    background — and for gold the enrichment runs to a thousand-fold. (3) Resources are
    mineralisation with reasonable prospects of eventual economic extraction; reserves are
    the part proven economically, technically and legally mineable (measured or indicated,
    above cut-off). Mine permits and financing rest on reserves, not resources.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>"Ore" is an economic definition — metal concentrated enough to extract at a profit — so reserves move with prices and technology.</li>
    <li>Crustal metals sit at parts per million; ore deposits concentrate them a hundred- to several-thousand-fold in rare pockets.</li>
    <li>Three engines do the concentrating: magmatic segregation, hydrothermal circulation, and sedimentary or surface processes.</li>
    <li>Exploration runs physics and chemistry first; drilling is the expensive confirmation of a survivor, not the search itself.</li>
    <li>Resources become reserves only through the confidence ladder (inferred, indicated, measured) plus proven modifying factors.</li>
    <li>The cut-off grade — one tonne of rock paying exactly for its own mining and processing — separates ore from waste.</li>
    <li>Grade is destiny: halving the grade halves revenue per tonne mined while costs barely move.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    With a deposit found and the reserve bankable, the discipline turns from <em>where</em>
    and <em>how much</em> to <em>how</em>: engineering the extraction and the processing that
    separates metal from rock, and — as the reconciliation work above shows — auditing every
    plan against what the mill actually receives.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cut-off grade is a moving policy, not a constant.</strong> Cut-off responds not only
    to price and cost but to <em>timing</em>: Lane's classic analysis showed that the
    profit-maximising cut-off changes over a mine's life, because milling capacity and market
    capacity constrain which tonnes are worth processing when. Running a low cut-off early
    starves the mill of grade; running it high strands low-grade rock that may never be mined.
    Grade-optimum policy is one of the few genuinely optimisable decisions in mining, and modern
    mine planners run it as software.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Reconciliation is the audit that ends every mine plan.</strong> Reserve models
    predict what the mill receives; production records report what it actually received. Persistent
    gaps between "predicted" and "actual" — too much dilution from waste rock mixed into ore,
    blast movement, unmodelled structures — are the industry's honest report card, and seasoned
    engineers read a company's reconciliation history before they read its resource statement.
    Dilution and mining recovery are usually the biggest blind spot of a fresh model: the
    economic reserve is <em>not</em> the geological reserve minus nothing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Grade decline is the quiet crisis of the field.</strong> Head grades at major copper
    mines have fallen from several percent a century ago to roughly half a percent, because we
    mined the rich deposits first. Lower grade means more rock per tonne of metal: energy, water,
    waste and cost per kilogram all climb. This arithmetic drives the push toward coarse particle
    flotation, ore sorting, in-situ leaching and better geometallurgy — and it explains why the
    discovery rate for major deposits has fallen even as exploration spending rose: what is
    left is deeper, under cover, or subtle.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The deposit model is the unifying idea.</strong> Every method above — geology,
    geophysics, geochemistry, economics — works because deposits of a type look alike: porphyries
    come in magnetic, potassium-altered, sulphide-halo packages; VMS bodies are conductive plates
    under conductive seafloor facies; Carlin gold hides in silicified limestone with arsenic and
    antimony haloes. Learn one deposit model and you have learned where to look, what to measure
    and what a reserve should look like — which is why "what deposit type am I in?" is the first
    question a mining engineer asks at any project, and the last one the geology ever stops
    answering.
  </p>

